= Outline

== Short introduction (<=2 min / <=1 page)

You play as a cat trying to escape an enclosed city.
  - All humans appear to have died out long ago
  - Inhabited only by companion robots
  - Robots have filled in the role as the residents of the city
    - Robots being representative of humans is a core thematic element (not the focus of our paper)

== Thesis
The use of diegetic music in _Stray_ helps to humanize the companion robots by 
showing them engaging in typically human musical activity while also highlighting
the "robotlike" qualities of their unique culture.

The robot charcters are not uncanney because they effeciently stradle the line between human and robot.

== (paper only) How game music supports believability of game narrative
Diegetic music concives the player that their actions take place within a narrative @hsw
Cognitive immersion means that being stiulated by lots of gane sound helps the player to be immersed
and tune out non-game noise @mivg. Diegetic music is congitivly immersive because the player
accepts the diegetic music as the true sonic background, not the noise of the real world.
By immersing the player in the narrative world, diegetic music is able to convince the player
of features of said world.

== Slums guitarist "Morusque"
Self insert by game composer @sn

Immitation of human musical practice @avsd

Sheet music titles look like robot versions of real music

Midi guitar sound creates a sense of uncertainty in whether the guitar is accoustic/synthesized

"Untitled" is random sounding and completly unplayable by a human, but Morusque doesn't
seem confused by it.

Robots have integrated the human culture of music performance into their own culture while
containing artifacts of their synthetic non-human nature.
The guitarists characterization as an "artist" strengthens the believability of this 
robot culture.

== Radio/jukebox
Interaction has an immersive effect @avsd.

Immersion reinforces the believability of "robot music".

Much of the music sounds like human genres, but some music sounds
glitchy and random. Robots appear to hear glitchy sounds just as regular music.

Radios and jukeboxes in locations like bars show a snapshot of the unique
musical culture that the robots have adapted to themselves. The interactivity
and immersive nature of these radios makes it believabile that such a culture exists


== Midtown dancers / nightclub
Robotic dance music heakens to human dance music which is often
characteried as robotic. (Daft Punk / Nightclub DJs) @sdam

Implied use of sampling/remixing by robot musicians implies the existance
of a unique hip-hop/underground culture. This causes the player to associate these
characters with human members of similar musical culture. Reinforced by associations
with dancers

